Q: How to delete a line in a string A: <p>A simple approach would be to do the following:</p>
<ol>
<li>Split the string using <code>lines()</code>.</li>
<li>Convert to a <code>Vec</code> using <code>collect()</code>;</li>
<li>Remove the line (which is an element in the vector) by index using <code>remove()</code>.</li>
<li>Convert back to string using <code>join()</code>.</li>
</ol>
<p>This is shown below:</p>
<pre><code>fn main() {
    let mut string = String::from(&quot;Hello\nWorld&quot;);

    let line_idx_to_remove = 1;

    let mut lines: Vec&lt;_&gt; = string.lines().collect();
    lines.remove(line_idx_to_remove);
    string = lines.join(&quot;\n&quot;);

    println!(&quot;{}&quot;, string);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Output:</p>
<pre><code>Hello
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Notes:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Your <code>string</code> should be marked mutable (<code>mut</code>) if you want to modify it.</li>
<li>In your real code you should verify <code>line_idx_to_remove</code> is a valid index.</li>
</ol>
<p><kbd><a href="https://play.rust-lang.org/?version=stable&amp;mode=debug&amp;edition=2024&amp;gist=260ef5e751714ed6a81981c736f0c186" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Live demo 1 - Rust Playground</a></kbd></p>
<p>Alternatively, you can use the following approach which is perhaps more idiomatic (but maybe a little less straightforward):</p>
<pre><code>fn main() {
    let string = String::from(&quot;Hello\nWorld&quot;);

    let line_idx_to_remove = 1;

    let string = string
        .lines()
        .enumerate()
        .filter(|(i, _)| *i != line_idx_to_remove)
        .map(|(_, line)| line)
        .collect::&lt;Vec&lt;_&gt;&gt;()
        .join(&quot;\n&quot;);

    println!(&quot;{}&quot;, string);
}
</code></pre>
<p><kbd><a href="https://play.rust-lang.org/?version=stable&amp;mode=debug&amp;edition=2024&amp;gist=fd477706120d7c1012a1859307d34259" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Live demo 2 - Rust Playground</a></kbd></p>

